Changes:
1. Disabled AutoCommit in PostgreSQL (https://github.com/ClickHouse/clickhouse-jdbc-bridge/issues/161):
This change is required for stream processing.
2. Added boolean-to-string conversion for PostgreSQL:
This change prevents the error: Caused by: org.postgresql.util.PSQLException: Bad value for type int: t when querying boolean types.
For example:
SELECT * FROM jdbc('jdbc:postgresql://host:5432/microsoft_contoso_integration_100k?user=postgres&password=', 'SELECT true');
3. Fixed precision loss for numeric types (https://github.com/ClickHouse/clickhouse-jdbc-bridge/issues/160):
This resolves issues where precision was lost for numeric values.
For example:
SELECT * FROM jdbc('jdbc:postgresql://host:5432/microsoft_contoso_integration_100k?user=postgres&password=', 'SELECT 123.456789');
4. Enabled HTTP compression:
Compression has been enabled for future use.
5. Minor refactoring of the DataTableReader->process method:
This refactor slightly improves performance for wide tables.
6. Disabled useless and frequent exceptions in logs from io.vertx.core.impl.BlockedThreadChecker.
7. Increased DEFAULT_FETCH_SIZE from 1000 to 10,000:
This increase has shown to improve efficiency by 20-30% in our cases.
8. Upgraded Java runtime from 1.8 to 21 LTS:
Updated the project to use Java 21 LTS (Eclipse Temurin) for improved performance, security, and modern JVM features.
Maven plugins were updated to compatible versions: compiler-plugin (3.12.1), surefire-plugin (3.2.5), failsafe-plugin (3.2.5), shade-plugin (3.5.1).
Docker images now use eclipse-temurin:21-jre base image instead of adoptopenjdk/openjdk8-openj9.
JVM options were modernized: replaced OpenJ9-specific flags with HotSpot optimizations (UseG1GC, UseStringDeduplication, ExitOnOutOfMemoryError).
This upgrade provides better garbage collection, enhanced security, and improved overall application performance.
9. Updated Caffeine cache library from 2.9.3 to 3.2.1:
Improved compatibility with Java 21 LTS and full GraalVM Native Image support.
Includes performance optimizations, bug fixes, and security updates.
The latest version is officially supported by GraalVM Native Image compilation.
10. Updated PostgreSQL JDBC Driver to 42.7.7:
Updated from various older versions (42.2.24, 42.3.4, 42.7.5) to the latest stable release.
Provides enhanced security, improved Java 21 compatibility, and better performance.
Includes fixes for connection handling, prepared statements optimization, and SSL improvements.
The driver is fully compatible with GraalVM Native Image and modern PostgreSQL server versions.
11. Updated HikariCP connection pool from 3.4.5 to 5.1.0:
Improved Java 21 LTS compatibility and better GraalVM Native Image support.
Enhanced performance monitoring, faster connection management, and updated security patches.
Provides better connection pool metrics and improved resource management.
12. Updated dnsjava library from 3.5.0 to 3.6.3:
Enhanced DNS resolution functionality with improved Java 21 LTS compatibility.
Includes critical security fixes for CVE-2024-25638 and CVE-2023-50387 (KeyTrap vulnerabilities).
Provides better DNSSEC support, improved query performance, and enhanced error handling.
Fixed various memory leaks and concurrency issues in DNS resolution operations.
The update brings full JPMS (Java Platform Module System) support and optimized resource management.
13. Updated SLF4J logging framework from 1.7.36 to 2.0.17:
Major version upgrade with improved logging performance and modernized architecture.
Enhanced Service Provider Interface (SPI) for better logging backend integration.
Provides backward compatibility for existing logging code while offering new features.
Includes optimized memory usage, faster logging operations, and improved thread safety.
Full compatibility with Java 21 LTS and better integration with modern logging frameworks.
The update brings enhanced debugging capabilities and more efficient log message formatting.
14. Introduced Vert.x BOM (Bill of Materials) for centralized dependency management:
Added Vert.x BOM (vertx-dependencies) to dependencyManagement section for consistent version control across all Vert.x modules.
This approach eliminates version conflicts and ensures compatibility between different Vert.x components.
All Vert.x dependencies now inherit versions from the BOM, removing the need to specify individual versions.
Provides better dependency management, reduces maintenance overhead, and prevents version mismatches.
The BOM ensures that all Vert.x modules use compatible versions tested together by the Vert.x team.
15. Updated Vert.x framework from 3.9.13 to 4.5.1 with breaking changes adaptations:
Major version upgrade from Vert.x 3.x to 4.x series with significant improvements in performance and functionality.
Updated all Vert.x modules: vertx-core, vertx-config, vertx-web, vertx-web-client, and vertx-micrometer-metrics.
Adapted codebase to handle breaking changes in Vert.x 4.x API, including updated method signatures and configuration patterns.
Enhanced HTTP server configuration, improved routing mechanisms, and better metrics integration.
The upgrade provides better Java 21 LTS compatibility, enhanced security features, and improved async processing.
Includes performance optimizations, modernized event loop handling, and better resource management.
Full compatibility with GraalVM Native Image compilation and reduced memory footprint in containerized environments.